  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. resource_efficiency.threshold
       Q: Is there a maximum throughput rate or resource-consumption ceiling (e.g., machine utilisation ≤ 90 %, labour intensity ≤ standard-pace × 1.1) beyond which you would not want the optimiser to push, even when fatigue and noise hard constraints are technically satisfied? Without a cap, a 'maximise' objective on an unbounded KPI can legally drive the system into unsustainable, near-limit operating conditions.
       A: Cap machine utilisation at 85 % and labour pace multiplier at 1.05× standard rate; resource_efficiency is therefore optimised only within this feasible envelope.
    2. context.horizon / shift_duration
       Q: What is the planned shift duration in hours (e.g., 8 h, 10 h, 12 h), and does that figure include scheduled rest breaks or only net productive time? Shift length directly determines fatigue accumulation rates and whether the ≤ 0.4 fatigue ceiling is achievable at any positive efficiency level.
       A: Assume an 8-hour shift with two 15-minute rest breaks and one 30-minute meal break, yielding 7 hours of net productive time; all fatigue-model parameters are scaled to this horizon.
    3. resource_efficiency / aggregation_weights
       Q: How should labour-hours, machine-hours, energy, and raw-material inputs be combined into a single resource-efficiency denominator — equal weight, internal cost rates, or energy-primary weighting? Different schemes can make opposite decisions optimal (e.g., substituting cheap energy for expensive labour versus conserving energy at the cost of overtime).
       A: Apply cost-based weighting using the plant's standard internal cost rates for each resource category; rates are sourced from the ERP system at shift start.
    4. operator_fatigue_index / effective_operating_target
       Q: Should the optimiser treat the hard ceiling of 0.4 as an ordinary operating point (running fatigue at 0.38–0.40 when efficiency demands it), or should it pursue a lower soft target (e.g., ≤ 0.30) and only allow up to 0.40 under unavoidable peak load? Your stated intent is to keep fatigue 'low', which suggests the ceiling is a safety backstop rather than a design target.
       A: Introduce a soft fatigue target of ≤ 0.30 as a secondary KPI objective; the hard constraint of ≤ 0.40 remains inviolable, and any schedule that breaches 0.30 is penalised in the objective function.
    5. priority_order / HumanWellbeing vs resource_efficiency (margin conflict)
       Q: When resource efficiency and human wellbeing conflict at the margin — for example, the highest-efficiency schedule holds fatigue at 0.39 and noise at 79 dB, just inside both hard constraints — should the optimiser accept that schedule, or should it sacrifice some efficiency to drive fatigue and noise meaningfully below their ceilings? The contract lists HumanWellbeing first in priority_order but gives no penalty for operating near (rather than at) the limits.
