    1. resource_efficiency / hard_constraints
       Q: Is there a minimum acceptable delivered output volume (e.g. ≥ 90% of shift baseline) to prevent the optimiser from achieving a high efficiency ratio simply by running at very low throughput — for example, processing only 20% of normal volume but at very low resource cost?
       A: Apply a minimum throughput floor of 85% of the shift baseline delivered-output-value so the optimiser cannot game the ratio by underproducing; add a hard constraint: delivered_output_value >= 0.85 * shift_baseline_output.
    2. resource_efficiency_formula (resource weighting)
       Q: How should the four resource types (labor, energy, raw materials, machine time) be weighted when summed into 'total_resources_consumed' — equal normalised weight, or do cost-based or physical-unit weights apply (e.g. energy counts 3× because it is the binding constraint on this line)?
       A: Use equal normalised weights (each resource scaled to its shift-baseline value) so no single resource dominates the denominator; document this weighting in the contract assumptions and flag for manager review before first run.
    3. priority_order vs _raw_priority_order
       Q: The formal priority list contains only 'HumanWellbeing', but the raw ordering puts resource_efficiency first and operator_fatigue second — once fatigue ≤ 0.4 and noise ≤ 80 dB are satisfied, should resource_efficiency be maximised without any further human-wellbeing preference, or should the optimiser still prefer solutions that further reduce fatigue even at a small efficiency cost (e.g. accept up to a 5% efficiency reduction to lower fatigue from 0.40 to 0.30)?
       A: Treat HumanWellbeing constraints as inviolable hard bounds (fatigue ≤ 0.4, noise ≤ 80 dB) and then maximise resource_efficiency unconstrained within that feasible region; add a note that any further wellbeing preference requires an explicit secondary objective weight.
    4. context.optimization_horizon (shift_duration)
       Q: What is the exact shift duration in hours — e.g. 8 h standard, 10 h extended, or 12 h continuous — given that the fatigue accumulation model and the noise TWA calculation are both time-dependent and a 12 h shift at the same pace roughly doubles fatigue exposure relative to an 8 h shift?
       A: Assume an 8-hour shift; scale the fatigue index accumulation rate and noise TWA window accordingly; flag for correction before deployment on any non-standard shift pattern.
    5. noise_level threshold (measurement method)
       Q: Does the 80 dB limit apply only to the continuous A-weighted time-weighted average (TWA) over the shift, or must instantaneous/short-term peaks also stay below a separate ceiling (e.g. OSHA 115 dB peak limit) — since a TWA of 80 dB is consistent with brief peaks well above 100 dB that could cause acute hearing damage?
       A: Apply the 80 dB cap to the continuous TWA as specified, and additionally enforce an instantaneous peak hard constraint of ≤ 110 dB based on standard occupational-health guidelines; add this peak constraint to hard_constraints if not already captured by the plant's machinery envelope.

