    1. resource_efficiency / kpi_targets[0]
       Q: Is there a target ceiling for resource_efficiency beyond which further gains are not worth pursuing (e.g., stop optimising once efficiency exceeds 1.2× baseline), or should the optimiser push efficiency to the absolute limit permitted by the fatigue and noise constraints?
       A: No saturation ceiling is applied; the optimiser maximises resource_efficiency up to the boundary of the two hard constraints. This may result in sustained near-limit operation (fatigue ≈ 0.39, noise ≈ 79.5 dB) for the entire shift.
    2. context / optimization_horizon (shift_duration)
       Q: What is the planned shift length in hours? The ambient_noise_level limit of 80 dB(A) is calibrated to an 8-hour OSHA exposure; a 10-hour shift requires ≈75 dB(A) and a 12-hour shift ≈73 dB(A) to remain compliant — should the noise threshold be adjusted accordingly?
       A: Shift duration is assumed to be exactly 8 hours, and the 80 dB(A) limit is applied without adjustment. If the actual shift is longer, the contract will be under-protective for noise exposure.
    3. assumptions / resource_definition (aggregation weights)
       Q: Resource consumption combines energy, raw materials, and machine utilisation time — what relative weights should be used to form a single scalar (e.g., 50% energy, 30% materials, 20% machine time)? Different weightings can favour completely different operating modes and shift the optimal point by ±20–30%.
       A: Equal weights (33/33/33) are applied to energy, raw materials, and machine utilisation time. This may over-penalise material waste or under-penalise energy spikes depending on actual cost structure.
    4. operator_fatigue_index / constraint temporality
       Q: Is the fatigue index ≤ 0.4 enforced as an instantaneous hard ceiling at every 5-minute sample, or as a rolling time-average over the shift? Instantaneous enforcement prevents any transient spike; rolling-average enforcement would allow brief exceedances during peak tasks provided recovery periods bring the mean back down.
       A: The constraint is enforced instantaneously at every measurement interval (≤ 5 min). No transient exceedance above 0.4 is permitted, even if the rolling average remains compliant.
    5. assumptions / resource_definition (delivered value — quality-throughput trade-off)
       Q: Delivered value is expressed as 'throughput-adjusted quality units' — what is the exchange rate between throughput rate and quality yield (e.g., does a 10% increase in throughput offset a 5% rise in defect rate)? Without this, the optimiser cannot determine whether to favour speed or precision when they conflict.
       A: Delivered value is computed as throughput_rate × (1 − defect_rate), giving equal multiplicative weight to volume and quality. A 10% defect rate reduces delivered value by 10% regardless of throughput gains.

